80655.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   23838 Nov 11 08:06 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 08:07 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     280 Nov 11 08:07 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    3212 Nov 11 08:09 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   12902 Nov 11 08:30 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   12949 Nov 11 08:26 references.pre_postdraft.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   20095 Nov 11 08:26 references.pre_postdraft.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   32933 Nov 11 08:30 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      16 Nov 11 08:06 results -> ../investigation\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:25 scientific-evidence\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:20 source_evidence\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 08:12 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     272 Nov 11 08:12 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent       7 Nov 11 08:06 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026 single-column, 8–9 body pages)\n\n**Status:** ready (bounded claim). **Prior paper story:** rejected (not edit mode; fresh framing).\n\n## Title (provisional)\nWhen Is One Kernel SHAP Run Enough? Predicting Top-3 Attribution Reproducibility from a Single Budgeted Run\n\n## Central question\nFor a trained tabular classifier explained by a budget-limited Kernel SHAP run, how often does the single run's top-3 feature set agree with independent reruns, and can that agreement be predicted from the run's own coalition data better than trivial and split-half baselines?\n\n## Central thesis\nSingle-run top-3 reproducibility is conditional: mean agreement is 0.807 at a 4d coalition budget and 0.932 at 16d (final evaluation). A diagnostic computed only from the assessed run beats always-reliable, constant-0.5 and split-half-gap baselines on squared error, but only modestly beats a pairing-permuted control (0.9838 vs 0.9701), so most of its skill is budget-level calibration. Per-instance resolution is limited (Spearman 0.58).\n\n## Why this framing\n- The final evaluation completed with outcome **qualified**. Lower bounds are positive against all three baselines and against the permuted control.\n- The artifacts do **not** support the broad brief. There is no per-dataset or per-model breakdown, no background-size or retraining-noise study, no deep tabular models and no ablations. These are stated as not done.\n- The honest story is therefore narrow: define reproducibility operationally, show it depends on budget, evaluate a single-run self-diagnostic against baselines and a control, and say plainly that the gain over the control is small.\n- Search chronology is not the story. Three configurations were scored on the search datasets (3 of 3 scored, none unscored). Scores are 0.9760, 0.9837 and 0.9854, and the per-seed ranges overlap. With no component ablations, no mechanism is attributed. This goes in the supplement.\n\n## Method groups\n1. **Reproducibility label (protocol):** mean top-3 Jaccard between the assessed run and 8 reruns; budgets 4d and 16d; 32-row background; logistic regression, random forest and MLP.\n2. **Single-run self-diagnostic (frozen selected configuration, proposed):** delete-one-group jackknife over complement-pair folds, 4 partitions, antithetic common-random-number draws, shrinkage toward a budget prior, small margin gate. Constants are hand-set and untuned.\n3. **Evaluator baselines:** always-reliable, constant 0.5, split-half-gap heuristic.\n4. **Permuted-pairing control:** 20 derangements; isolates instance-specific information.\n5. **Search-plane variants (supplement only).**\n\nThe original search-time baseline program has no grounded score in `paper_data.json`, so that comparison is omitted.\n\n## Results focus (all from `final.*`, search kept separate)\n- Label means 0.8065 (4d) vs 0.9315 (16d).\n- Score 0.9838 (= 1 − MSE, so MSE ≈ 0.0162, derived). Seed scores are 0.9850 (seed 9001) and 0.9826 (seed 9002).\n- Skill over always-reliable is +0.0353 (lower bound 0.0171).\n- Skill over constant 0.5 is +0.1543 (lower bound 0.1245).\n- Skill over the split-half gap heuristic is +0.0122 (lower bound 0.0081).\n- Diagnostic minus control is 0.0137 (derived), lower bound 0.0101.\n- RMSE is about 0.127 vs about 0.227 for always-reliable (derived).\n- Only one-sided 2.5th-percentile bootstrap lower bounds exist; no upper bounds.\n\n## Figures (single-column template, text width ≈ 5.5 in; both full width)\n- **Fig. 1 `protocol_overview`:** concept figure (generate-concept-figure), page 1–2: model and instance → one run at 4d or 16d → r_hat; evaluator reruns ×8 → Jaccard label; scoring against baselines and control. 1 panel.\n- **Fig. 2 `main_comparison`:** matplotlib, 2 panels. (a) Skill over the three baselines and over the permuted control, with lower-bound markers (4 categories). (b) Mean agreement at 4d vs 16d (2 categories). PDF in LaTeX plus 300-DPI PNG.\n- **Table 1:** native LaTeX, final metrics, lower bounds, seed scores, derived columns marked.\n- The supplement has a small table or figure of the three search-plane scores.\n\n## Supplement\n- Protocol and reproducibility details: datasets, models, 20 instances, 8 reruns, 20 derangements, 200 bootstrap replicates, seeds 9001/9002, software versions, disjointness.\n- Full metric table and derivations.\n- Search-plane configurations, with no mechanism claims.\n- Diagnostic constants and provenance, including that the budget prior came from search-plane label means (0.840/0.958) that differ from the final ones.\n\n## Body-page use\nNo padding. Space goes to framing, formal definitions, an implementable method section, protocol rationale, results and Table 1, a sharp limitations section, and related work. If the evidence cannot honestly fill the 8-page minimum, flag the shortfall rather than padding.\n\n## Forbidden headings and phrases\n- **Headings:** search progression, milestones, first valid result, campaign phases, cycle-based headings, Experiment A/B, run log.\n- **Phrases:** \"correctness rate\", \"correct and eligible\", \"combined_score\", \"eligible\", \"rubric\", \"state of the art\", \"incumbent\", and blanket claims that SHAP is or is not reliable.\n\n## Writer cautions\n- Per-dataset and per-family claims are not grounded.\n- Diagnostics trust is marked unverified, and the constants are untuned.\n- `references.bib` is mostly off-topic. Usable entries: Covert2020ImprovingKP, Gupte2025UnderstandingTB and Mitchell2021SamplingPF, possibly Petrosian2024ExplainableAE. The starter references (Lundberg and Lee, Krishna, Nogueira, Webber, Musco and Witter, Chen, Ding) are missing, so re-run retrieve-references for them or do not cite.\n- Anonymize the paper, include the LLM Usage section, and fill the checklist.\nanalysis:\nbuild_figures.py\nderive_metrics.py\nderived_metrics.json\nderived_metrics.json.prov.json\n\nfigures:\nerror_accounting.pdf\nerror_accounting.pdf.prov.json\nerror_accounting.png\nerror_accounting.png.prov.json\nmain_comparison.pdf\nmain_comparison.pdf.prov.json\nmain_comparison.png\nmain_comparison.png.prov.json\nprotocol_overview.png\nprotocol_overview.prompts.json\n\nscientific-evidence:\nbundle.manifest.json\nledger.jsonl\nschemas\nspec.md\nverification-report.json\n\nsource_evidence:\nevaluator_final.py\nfinal_manifest.json\nfinal_resources_manifest.json\nsearch_eval_evidence_selected.json\nsearch_resources_manifest.json\nsearchplane_config_A_program.py\nsearchplane_config_B_program.py\nselected_config_NOTES.md\nselected_config_program.py\ntask_contract.json","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18050b94-e857-4c8d-b7a8-a11a5c26f668","uuid":"ab959113-3853-4714-ba32-8a79c660dc18","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:30:11.868Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 4860\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:30 .\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:06 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     751 Nov 11 08:06 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:06 .claude\n-rw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 08:06 .paper_two_stage_enabled\nprw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 08:3