{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WkSmDernXQod1hJZL5Nbsy","type":"tool_result","content":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is well cited on Shapley estimation, value-function semantics, stability measures and tabular data/models. All 26 `\\citep` keys resolve in `references.bib`. Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and need no citations. Three narrow gaps justify a targeted retrieval pass.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Run-to-run stability and per-instance uncertainty of sampling-based local explainers (LIME/SHAP).\n- Delete-a-group jackknife variance estimation, and shrinkage/empirical-Bayes estimation.\n- Use of feature attributions in deployed tabular decision settings.\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. related-work-instability-comparators (high, comparator) — Related Work\n- **Anchor:** \"Local explanations are known to vary under perturbation and between methods ... Our label isolates a different and narrower source of variation\".\n- **Why:** The paper's core claim is a per-run reproducibility estimate for a stochastic explainer. The related work cites only generic robustness and disagreement papers, plus population-level uncertainty (Williamson2020Efficient). It does not cite work that measures repeated-run instability or per-instance sampling uncertainty of LIME/SHAP-style estimators. These are the closest comparators, and the novelty positioning is thin without them.\n- **Search topics:** stability indices and repeatability of LIME under random sampling; confidence intervals or standard errors for Kernel SHAP and sampled Shapley estimates; reliability of SHAP across repeated runs and budgets.\n\n### 2. method-jackknife-shrinkage-rationale (medium, background) — Method\n- **Anchor:** \"delete-one-group jackknife covariance\"; \"shrink the simulated value toward a budget-specific prior\".\n- **Why:** The diagnostic's two statistical ingredients have no cited source. Both topics were in the original query plan but did not reach the bibliography. One or two foundational references would ground the terminology without changing the story.\n- **Search topics:** delete-a-group jackknife variance estimation; empirical Bayes shrinkage toward a prior.\n\n### 3. intro-deployment-claim (low, positioning) — Introduction\n- **Anchor:** \"Shapley-value attributions are a standard way to justify an individual prediction ... in credit, medical and similar settings\".\n- **Why:** The deployment-practice claim (\"run the explainer once and report what it returns\") is supported only by the original SHAP paper.\n- **Search topics:** explainable ML in deployment, and how practitioners use feature attributions.\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Dataset citations (German Credit, Give Me Some Credit, Pima). The datasets are described with sources in the appendix, and extra citations would only add bibliography size.\n- Shapley estimation variants and value-function semantics, which are already well covered.\n- Deep tabular models, which are explicitly out of scope.\n# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. The gap memo found three narrow gaps in an otherwise well-cited draft. The plan has five queries, all tied to a specific gap and anchor text. Retrieval has not been run.\n\n**Central topic:** reliability of a single run of budget-limited stochastic Kernel SHAP for tabular top-k attributions, predicted per instance by a delete-one-group jackknife with budget-prior shrinkage.\n\n## Queries\n\n1. **Repeated-run stability of LIME explanations** (`baseline_comparator`, high priority)\n   - Query: `stability of LIME explanations repeated runs random perturbation sampling instability index`\n   - Gap: `related-work-instability-comparators`. Related Work cites only generic robustness and disagreement papers (Alvarez-Melis, Krishna, Yuan).\n   - Purpose: find the closest comparators that measure rerun repeatability of sampled explainers, so the \"narrower source of variation\" claim has something to be compared with.\n\n2. **Per-instance uncertainty for Kernel SHAP** (`baseline_comparator`, high priority)\n   - Query: `confidence intervals standard errors for Kernel SHAP sampled Shapley estimates per instance uncertainty`\n   - Gap: same as above. The claim \"a statement about the run rather than the estimator's average error\" is positioned only against population-level inference (Williamson2020Efficient) and worst-case guarantees (Musco2024Provably, Covert2020Improving).\n   - Purpose: cite per-instance sampling-uncertainty work for SHAP-style estimators.\n\n3. **Delete-a-group jackknife** (`core_method`, medium priority)\n   - Query: `delete-a-group jackknife variance estimation`\n   - Gap: `method-jackknife-shrinkage-rationale`. The \"delete-one-group jackknife covariance\" has no statistical source.\n   - Purpose: one or two foundational references to ground the terminology.\n\n4. **Empirical Bayes shrinkage** (`core_method`, medium priority)\n   - Query: `empirical Bayes shrinkage estimation toward a prior`\n   - Gap: same as above. The draft calls shrinkage toward a budget-specific prior \"empirical-Bayes-style\" without a source.\n   - Purpose: one foundational empirical Bayes or James-Stein reference.\n\n5. **Deployment use of attributions** (`benchmark_context`, low priority)\n   - Query: `explainable machine learning in deployment how practitioners use feature attributions`\n   - Gap: `intro-deployment-claim`. The credit and medical deployment claim, and the \"run once\" practice, rest only on Lundberg2017Unified.\n   - Purpose: one empirical deployment or practitioner study. Drop it if results are noisy.\n\n## Deliberately excluded\n- Dataset citations (German Credit, Give Me Some Credit, Pima). The appendix already gives sources, and the gap memo says not to add them.\n- Shapley estimator variants, value-function semantics, rank-similarity measures and deep tabular models. These are already covered or out of scope.\n- Any query on directions already in the bibliography: Covert2020Improving, Musco2024Provably, Mitchell2021Sampling, Nogueira2017Stability, Webber2010similarity, Yuan2022empirical, Chen2020True.\n[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 5 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/5 | stability of LIME explanations repeated runs random perturbation sampling instability index\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/25 (25 left) | candidates 0/0 | query 1/5\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 2/5 | confidence intervals standard errors for Kernel SHAP sampled Shapley estimates per instance uncertainty\n[semantic_scholar] search failed | query 2/5 | confidence intervals standard errors for Kernel SHAP sampled Shapley estimates per instance uncertainty | Semantic Scholar HTTP 429 for https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/search?query=confidence+intervals+standard+errors+for+Kernel+SHAP+sampled+Shapley+estimates+per+instance+uncertainty&limit=25&fields=paperId%2Ctitle%2Cyear%2Cvenue%2CcitationCount\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 3/5 | delete-a-group jackknife variance estimation\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/25 (25 left) | candidates 0/25 | query 3/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [------------] | refs 0/25 (25 left) | candidates 1/25 | query 3/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#-----------] | refs 1/25 (24 left) | candidates 2/25 | query 3/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#-----------] | refs 2/25 (23 left) | candidates 3/25 | query 3/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#-----------] | refs 3/25 (22 left) | candidates 4/25 | query 3/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [##----------] | refs 4/25 (21 left) | candidates 5/25 | query 3/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [##----------] | refs 5/25 (20 left) | candidates 6/25 | query 3/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [###---------] | refs 6/25 (19 left) | candidates 7/25 | query 3/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [###---------] | 