{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvpxphTv7HR3zL26jhFS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D6WuGHfpgNooxePkQbJdeq","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n 'section\\|\\\\cite' paper.tex | head -80; wc -w paper.tex; python3 -c \"\nimport json;a=json.load(open('references_provenance.json'));print(a['queries']);print(a['generated_at']);print([ (r['id'],r.get('bibtex_key')) for r in a['references']][:5])\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":993,"cache_read_input_tokens":50000,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":993},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18050b94-e857-4c8d-b7a8-a11a5c26f668","uuid":"1bc39e11-8857-41f6-b3eb-ec02160f2f4c","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:30:23.540Z","request_id":"req_011CgvpxndwE9Pcs9UofxF2s"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6WuGHfpgNooxePkQbJdeq","type":"tool_result","content":"43:\\section{Introduction}\n44:Shapley-value attributions are a standard way to justify an individual prediction of a tabular model in credit, medical and similar settings \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified}. For models without a tractable exact algorithm, they are usually estimated by a stochastic procedure under a fixed budget of model evaluations. Two runs of the same estimator on the same trained model and the same instance therefore return different attribution vectors, and the features that appear in a short list of reasons can change from run to run. The common deployment practice is to run the explainer once and report what it returns. Whether that practice is adequate, and under which conditions, is rarely stated as a checkable claim.\n65:\\section{Related Work}\n67:Kernel SHAP casts Shapley estimation as a weighted regression over sampled coalitions \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified}. Later work reduces its variance through paired sampling and regression refinements \\citep{Covert2020Improving}, permutation sampling \\citep{Mitchell2021Sampling}, leverage-score sampling with error guarantees \\citep{Musco2024Provably}, and amortized estimators \\citep{Jethani2021FastSHAP}. \\citet{Gupte2025Understanding} compare Shapley approximations empirically on tabular data. These papers make the estimator more accurate or give accuracy guarantees at a chosen budget. We take the budget as given and ask what a user holding one run can say about the reproducibility of its top-3 set, which is a statement about the run rather than about the estimator's average error.\n70:The value function defines what is being attributed: interventional or conditional expectations answer different questions \\citep{Sundararajan2019many,Chen2020True,Frye2020Shapley,Kumar2020Problems,Covert2020Explaining}, and additive attributions can misrepresent models with interactions \\citep{Gosiewska2019Do}. We fix an interventional value function with a 32-row background and treat the resulting attribution, not its causal or data-faithful interpretation, as the object whose run-to-run stability is measured.\n73:Local explanations are known to vary under perturbation and between methods \\citep{Ribeiro2016Why,AlvarezMelis2018Robustness,Krishna2022Disagreement}, and \\citet{Yuan2022empirical} study how background size affects SHAP stability for deep models. Our label isolates a different and narrower source of variation, namely the coalition sampling of a single estimator with everything else fixed. This is analogous in spirit to predictive multiplicity, which asks how much predictions vary across equally good alternatives \\citep{Marx2019Predictive}, except that the alternatives here are reruns of an estimator.\n76:Feature-selection stability is commonly measured by average pairwise similarity of selected sets, with corrections for chance agreement \\citep{Nogueira2017Stability}, and rank-based comparisons of incomplete lists use measures such as rank-biased overlap \\citep{Webber2010similarity}. We use the plain Jaccard index on top-3 sets because three reasons is a natural unit for a decision explanation. We do not apply a chance correction, which matters when datasets with $d=10$ and $d=20$ are averaged (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}). Uncertainty quantification for Shapley-based importance has been developed at the population level \\citep{Williamson2020Efficient}; we address the per-instance, per-run level.\n79:Our held-out datasets include an ACS income task from the folktables toolkit \\citep{Ding2021Retiring}. Our model families are logistic regression, random forest and a small MLP implemented in scikit-learn \\citep{Pedregosa2012Scikit}. Deep tabular architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021Revisiting} and the comparison of tree ensembles with neural models \\citep{Grinsztajn2022tree} are not part of this study, and exact tree-specific Shapley algorithms \\citep{Lundberg2018Consistent} are not used. Our framing of reproducibility as a measurable property with explicit scope follows the call for rigorous evaluation of interpretability \\citep{DoshiVelez2017Towards}.\n81:\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n82:\\subsection{Problem setup}\n105:\\subsection{Design rationale}\n108:\\subsection{Compared predictors}\n128:\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n131:\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n132:All numbers in this section come from the sealed final evaluation unless stated otherwise. The evaluation completed and its recorded outcome is \\emph{qualified}. The pre-declared assessment rule from the protocol is met by the reported lower bounds, all four of which are positive. This is a statement about the diagnostic's measured skill in this regime, and not a validity, superiority or generality claim about Kernel SHAP reliability.\n134:\\subsection{Single-run reproducibility depends on the budget}\n146:\\subsection{The diagnostic against baselines and a control}\n174:\\subsection{How much is instance-specific?}\n188:\\subsection{Consistency across seeds and search-plane checks}\n193:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n204:The label captures only coalition-sampling noise. Background resampling \\citep{Yuan2022empirical}, retraining noise and disagreement between explainers \\citep{Krishna2022Disagreement} are not measured and would add to run-to-run variation in practice, so our reproducibility estimates are optimistic for a deployment where those vary. The label uses $R=8$ reruns, which makes it noisy; the diagnostic's measured MSE includes this label noise, so the true prediction error of $\\rhat$ for the conditional expectation could be smaller, and we cannot say by how much. Jaccard on top-3 sets is not corrected for chance agreement, whose level differs between $d=10$ and $d=20$ \\citep{Nogueira2017Stability}, and equal-weight dataset averaging mixes these regimes. Other values of $k$, rank-aware measures such as RBO \\citep{Webber2010similarity}, and top-1 agreement were not studied.\n212:\\section{Conclusion}\n223:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n225:\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\n247:\\subsection{Derived quantities}\n268:\\subsection{Selected configuration constants}\n290:\\subsection{Search-plane scores of the three configurations}\n309:\\subsection{Reproducibility statement}\n312:\\subsection{LLM usage}\n5204 paper.tex\n['Improving KernelSHAP: practical Shapley value estimation using linear regression', 'Shapley value estimation sampling error bounds approximation guarantees', 'uncertainty quantification confidence intervals for Shapley value estimates feature attribution', 'stability of post-hoc explanations LIME SHAP repeated runs random sampling instability', 'top-k feature attribution agreement metrics rank-biased overlap Jaccard rank correlation explanations', 'delete-a-group jackknife variance estimation', 'empirical Bayes shrinkage noisy variance estimates', 'interventional versus observational conditional expectation Shapley values background data choice', 'Statlog German Credit Pima Indians Diabetes tabular classification benchmark datasets']\n2026-11-11T08:07:41.278553+00:00\n[('ref.s2.3dd57363da3201f920c7e0b17a29e59ef44da724', None), ('ref.s2.3fc7625f169756de672bc60b97d322ede2b3e0cb', None), ('ref.openalex.W2618851150', None), ('ref.openalex.W4224936048', None), ('ref.openalex.W4403853934', None)]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18050b94-e857-4c8d-b7a8-a11a5c26f668","uuid":"7f700651-008f-4e0d-a3c3-80fe4a6a384e","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:30:23.617Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"43:\\section{Introduction}\n44:Shapley-value attributions are a standard way to justify an individual prediction of a tabular model in credit, medical and similar settings \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified}. For models without a tractable exact algorithm, they are usually estimated by a stochastic procedure under