ize affects SHAP stability for deep models. Our label isolates a different and narrower source of variation, namely the coalition sampling of a single estimator with everything else fixed. This is analogous in spirit to predictive multiplicity, which asks how much predictions vary across equally good alternatives \\citep{Marx2019Predictive}, except that the alternatives here are reruns of an estimator.\n76:Feature-selection stability is commonly measured by average pairwise similarity of selected sets, with corrections for chance agreement \\citep{Nogueira2017Stability}, and rank-based comparisons of incomplete lists use measures such as rank-biased overlap \\citep{Webber2010similarity}. We use the plain Jaccard index on top-3 sets because three reasons is a natural unit for a decision explanation. We do not apply a chance correction, which matters when datasets with $d=10$ and $d=20$ are averaged (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}). Uncertainty quantification for Shapley-based importance has been developed at the population level \\citep{Williamson2020Efficient}; we address the per-instance, per-run level.\n79:Our held-out datasets include an ACS income task from the folktables toolkit \\citep{Ding2021Retiring}. Our model families are logistic regression, random forest and a small MLP implemented in scikit-learn \\citep{Pedregosa2012Scikit}. Deep tabular architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021Revisiting} and the comparison of tree ensembles with neural models \\citep{Grinsztajn2022tree} are not part of this study, and exact tree-specific Shapley algorithms \\citep{Lundberg2018Consistent} are not used. Our framing of reproducibility as a measurable property with explicit scope follows the call for rigorous evaluation of interpretability \\citep{DoshiVelez2017Towards}.\n81:\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n82:\\subsection{Problem setup}\n105:\\subsection{Design rationale}\n108:\\subsection{Compared predictors}\n128:\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n131:\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n132:All numbers in this section come from the sealed final evaluation unless stated otherwise. The evaluation completed and its recorded outcome is \\emph{qualified}. The pre-declared assessment rule from the protocol is met by the reported lower bounds, all four of which are positive. This is a statement about the diagnostic's measured skill in this regime, and not a validity, superiority or generality claim about Kernel SHAP reliability.\n134:\\subsection{Single-run reproducibility depends on the budget}\n146:\\subsection{The diagnostic against baselines and a control}\n174:\\subsection{How much is instance-specific?}\n188:\\subsection{Consistency across seeds and search-plane checks}\n193:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n204:The label captures only coalition-sampling noise. Background resampling \\citep{Yuan2022empirical}, retraining noise and disagreement between explainers \\citep{Krishna2022Disagreement} are not measured and would add to run-to-run variation in practice, so our reproducibility estimates are optimistic for a deployment where those vary. The label uses $R=8$ reruns, which makes it noisy; the diagnostic's measured MSE includes this label noise, so the true prediction error of $\\rhat$ for the conditional expectation could be smaller, and we cannot say by how much. Jaccard on top-3 sets is not corrected for chance agreement, whose level differs between $d=10$ and $d=20$ \\citep{Nogueira2017Stability}, and equal-weight dataset averaging mixes these regimes. Other values of $k$, rank-aware measures such as RBO \\citep{Webber2010similarity}, and top-1 agreement were not studied.\n212:\\section{Conclusion}\n223:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n225:\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\n247:\\subsection{Derived quantities}\n268:\\subsection{Selected configuration constants}\n290:\\subsection{Search-plane scores of the three configurations}\n309:\\subsection{Reproducibility statement}\n312:\\subsection{LLM usage}\n5204 paper.tex\n['Improving KernelSHAP: practical Shapley value estimation using linear regression', 'Shapley value estimation sampling error bounds approximation guarantees', 'uncertainty quantification confidence intervals for Shapley value estimates feature attribution', 'stability of post-hoc explanations LIME SHAP repeated runs random sampling instability', 'top-k feature attribution agreement metrics rank-biased overlap Jaccard rank correlation explanations', 'delete-a-group jackknife variance estimation', 'empirical Bayes shrinkage noisy variance estimates', 'interventional versus observational conditional expectation Shapley values background data choice', 'Statlog German Credit Pima Indians Diabetes tabular classification benchmark datasets']\n2026-11-11T08:07:41.278553+00:00\n[('ref.s2.3dd57363da3201f920c7e0b17a29e59ef44da724', None), ('ref.s2.3fc7625f169756de672bc60b97d322ede2b3e0cb', None), ('ref.openalex.W2618851150', None), ('ref.openalex.W4224936048', None), ('ref.openalex.W4403853934', None)]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
