{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L2WpSrFJ97Z7MRPJf3Fb3p","type":"tool_result","content":"\\section{Introduction}\nShapley-value attributions are a standard way to justify an individual prediction of a tabular model in credit, medical and similar settings \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified}. For models without a tractable exact algorithm, they are usually estimated by a stochastic procedure under a fixed budget of model evaluations. Two runs of the same estimator on the same trained model and the same instance therefore return different attribution vectors, and the features that appear in a short list of reasons can change from run to run. The common deployment practice is to run the explainer once and report what it returns. Whether that practice is adequate, and under which conditions, is rarely stated as a checkable claim.\n\nWe study a deliberately narrow version of this question. The explainer is Kernel SHAP with an interventional value function and a 32-row background, run with a budget of $B=4d$ or $B=16d$ coalitions for a model with $d$ features. The decision-relevant object is the top-3 feature set by absolute attribution. We call a single run \\emph{reproducible} to the degree that independent reruns, which differ only in their coalition sampling, return the same top-3 set. This definition ignores background resampling, model retraining and the choice between explainers; those sources of disagreement are outside our scope and we do not measure them.\n\nThe study has two parts. First, we measure how reproducible single runs are at the two budgets, on three tabular datasets and three model families that were held out from the selection of the diagnostic. Second, we ask whether reproducibility can be \\emph{predicted for a given run from that run alone}. A practitioner holds one run, not eight. A self-diagnostic that reads the run's coalition masks, weights and evaluated coalition values and returns a reproducibility estimate $\\rhat\\in[0,1]$ would let the practitioner flag instances whose top-3 set should not be trusted. We evaluate such a diagnostic against three baselines and against a control that keeps all episode-level information but destroys instance-level pairing.\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Shapley estimation.}\nKernel SHAP casts Shapley estimation as a weighted regression over sampled coalitions \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified}. Later work reduces its variance through paired sampling and regression refinements \\citep{Covert2020Improving}, permutation sampling \\citep{Mitchell2021Sampling}, leverage-score sampling with error guarantees \\citep{Musco2024Provably}, and amortized estimators \\citep{Jethani2021FastSHAP}. \\citet{Gupte2025Understanding} compare Shapley approximations empirically on tabular data. These papers make the estimator more accurate or give accuracy guarantees at a chosen budget. We take the budget as given and ask what a user holding one run can say about the reproducibility of its top-3 set, which is a statement about the run rather than about the estimator's average error.\n\n\\paragraph{What a Shapley value means.}\nThe value function defines what is being attributed: interventional or conditional expectations answer different questions \\citep{Sundararajan2019many,Chen2020True,Frye2020Shapley,Kumar2020Problems,Covert2020Explaining}, and additive attributions can misrepresent models with interactions \\citep{Gosiewska2019Do}. We fix an interventional value function with a 32-row background and treat the resulting attribution, not its causal or data-faithful interpretation, as the object whose run-to-run stability is measured.\n\n\\paragraph{Instability and disagreement of explanations.}\nLocal explanations are known to vary under perturbation and between methods \\citep{Ribeiro2016Why,AlvarezMelis2018Robustness,Krishna2022Disagreement}, and \\citet{Yuan2022empirical} study how background size affects SHAP stability for deep models. Our label isolates a different and narrower source of variation, namely the coalition sampling of a single estimator with everything else fixed. This is analogous in spirit to predictive multiplicity, which asks how much predictions vary across equally good alternatives \\citep{Marx2019Predictive}, except that the alternatives here are reruns of an estimator.\n\n\\paragraph{Stability and similarity measures.}\nFeature-selection stability is commonly measured by average pairwise similarity of selected sets, with corrections for chance agreement \\citep{Nogueira2017Stability}, and rank-based comparisons of incomplete lists use measures such as rank-biased overlap \\citep{Webber2010similarity}. We use the plain Jaccard index on top-3 sets because three reasons is a natural unit for a decision explanation. We do not apply a chance correction, which matters when datasets with $d=10$ and $d=20$ are averaged (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}). Uncertainty quantification for Shapley-based importance has been developed at the population level \\citep{Williamson2020Efficient}; we address the per-instance, per-run level.\n\n\\paragraph{Tabular data and models.}\nOur held-out datasets include an ACS income task from the folktables toolkit \\citep{Ding2021Retiring}. Our model families are logistic regression, random forest and a small MLP implemented in scikit-learn \\citep{Pedregosa2012Scikit}. Deep tabular architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021Revisiting} and the comparison of tree ensembles with neural models \\citep{Grinsztajn2022tree} are not part of this study, and exact tree-specific Shapley algorithms \\citep{Lundberg2018Consistent} are not used. Our framing of reproducibility as a measurable property with explicit scope follows the call for rigorous evaluation of interpretability \\citep{DoshiVelez2017Towards}.\n\nThe label is conditional on the particular assessed run, so it can in principle be predicted from that run's data. It measures coalition-sampling noise only. Because it uses $R=8$ reruns it is itself a noisy estimate of the conditional expectation, and we cannot quantify that noise from the retained evidence.\n\n\\paragraph{Diagnostic task.}\nThe diagnostic receives, for each of up to 20 explained instances of a trained model, the assessed run's masks, weights, evaluated values and anchors, plus $d$, $B$, $k=3$ and the model-family name. It returns $\\rhat\\in[0,1]$ per instance. It never sees labels, reruns, model internals or dataset names.\n\n\\subsection{Design rationale}\nKernel SHAP is a weighted regression, so run-to-run variation of $\\phi$ is sampling variation of regression coefficients. The top-3 set changes between runs when this variation is large relative to the gap between the third and fourth largest $|\\phi_j|$. A single run contains information about its own sampling variation: deleting random subsets of its coalition pairs and re-solving the regression shows how sensitive $\\phi$ is to the particular coalitions drawn. Propagating that sensitivity to the top-3 set by simulation gives an estimate of the expected overlap with a rerun, which is what the label measures. Two weaknesses motivate the remaining components. The jackknife is computed from only $B/2$ coalition pairs and is itself noisy, particularly at $4d$; and its spread need not match the rerun spread. We therefore shrink the simulated value toward a budget-specific prior. We hypothesized that this would reduce error most where the label is high and nearly constant (large budget) and that instance-specific information would matter most near ties between the third and fourth feature. We did not run component ablations, so these are design motivations and not tested mechanisms.\n\n\\subsection{Compared predictors}\n\\paragraph{Self-diagnostic (frozen selected configuration).}\nFor each instance, with $F=8$ folds and $P=4$ independent random partitions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Assign complement pairs of coalitions at random to folds, drop one fold at a time, and re-solve the regression to obtain leave-fold-out solutions $\\phi^{(-f)}$. Folds that leave no more than $d$ rows are skipped. Define centered, scaled deviations $\\Delta_f=\\sqrt{(F'-1)/F'}\\,(\\phi^{(-f)}-\\bar\\phi^{(-\\cdot)})$ over the $F'$ valid folds, so that $\\sum_f\\Delta_f\\Delta_f^{\\top}$ is the delete-one-group jackknife covariance.\n\\item Simulate reruns with 1000 common-random-number draws (500 antithetic pairs, seeded by the episode seed). The diagonal simulator uses $\\phi+\\mu\\,\\sigma\\odot\\zeta$ with $\\sigma_j=(\\sum_f\\Delta_{fj}^2)^{1/2}$ and $\\zeta\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I_d)$. The full-covariance simulator uses $\\phi+\\sum_f\\zeta_f\\Delta_f$, which preserves the efficiency constraint. Each simulated top-3 set is compared with the observed one by Jaccard index and averaged. This gives $s_{d,1}$ (diagonal, $\\mu=1$), $s_{d,0.7}$ (diagonal, $\\mu=0.7$) and $s_{f,1}$ (full covariance, $\\mu=1$).\n\\item Compute the margin $z_{\\mathrm{m}}$, the gap between the third and fourth largest $|\\phi_j|$ divided by the jackknife standard error of that gap, averaged over partitions along with the three simulated values.\n\\item Combine: $s=0.5\\,s_{d,1}+0.2\\,s_{d,0.7}+0.3\\,s_{f,1}$ and\n\\begin{equation}\n\\rhat=\\mathrm{clip}_{[0.02,1]}\\Big(0.6\\,s+0.4\\,\\pi(B/d)+0.02\\tanh\\big(z_{\\mathrm m}/3\\big)\\Big),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere the prior $\\pi$ is linear in $\\log_2(B/d)$ between $0.84$ at $B=4d$ and $0.96$ at $B=16d$.\n\\end{enumerate}\nOn any numerical failure the output falls back to $\\pi(B/d)$. The source is in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/selected\\_config\\_program.py} (constants at lines 35--46; functions \\texttt{\\_jackknife\\_cov}, \\texttt{\\_sim\\_jaccard}, \\texttt{\\_margin\\_z}, \\texttt{\\_empirical\\_bayes} and \\texttt{\\_diagnose\\_one}). The final record stores only a tree hash of the evaluated sources, so the above describes the source file of the configuration designated as selected in the search record, not a hash-verified copy.\n\nProvenance of the constants matters for interpretation. The weights $0.5/0.2/0.3$, $0.6$, $0.02$, the fold count and the partition count are hand-set and were not tuned on instance labels. The values $0.84$ and $0.96$ are described in the configuration's notes as aggregate label means by budget from earlier search-plane evaluations (\\texttt{selected\\_config\\_NOTES.md}). The shrinkage weight is therefore a fixed constant, not an estimated empirical-Bayes weight; we use the term \\emph{empirical-Bayes-style} only for the structure of shrinking toward a data-derived prior.\n\n\\paragraph{Baselines and control.}\nAll are scored with the same per-instance squared error $(\\rhat_i-y_i)^2$. \\emph{Always reliable} outputs $\\rhat=1$. \\emph{Constant 0.5} outputs $0.5$. The \\emph{split-half gap} heuristic is computed by the evaluator from the assessed run alone: it solves the regression separately on the two halves of the complement-pair blocks, sets $se_j=|\\phi^A_j-\\phi^B_j|/2$, forms $z=(|\\phi|_{(3)}-|\\phi|_{(4)})/\\sqrt{se_{(3)}^2+se_{(4)}^2}$, and outputs $0.5+0.5\\tanh z$. The \\emph{pairing-permuted control} scores the diagnostic's own outputs against labels after permuting them among the 20 instances of an episode with each of 20 fixed derangements and averaging. It therefore keeps everything the diagnostic knows about the episode, such as its budget, model and dataset level, and removes only the instance-level pairing between $\\rhat$ and $y$. The diagnostic exceeds this control only if its instance-specific variation is informative.\n\n\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\nThe evaluation was run once under a sealed protocol, with the selected configuration frozen beforehand. Datasets are the ACS income task (California, 2018, 1-year person records via folktables; $d=10$; 20{,}000 sampled rows), Give Me Some Credit (OpenML 46929; $d=10$; 20{,}000 sampled rows) and the second half of a hash-based split of Statlog German Credit ($d=20$; 485 rows). Models are scikit-learn logistic regression, a 100-tree random forest and a one-hidden-layer MLP with 64 units, trained on a 70\\% split with train-only median imputation and standardization. For each dataset, family, budget in $\\{4d,16d\\}$ and seed in $\\{9001,9002\\}$ there is one episode of 20 explained test instances, with nine runs per instance (one assessed, eight reruns). That gives 36 episodes and 720 labeled instances. Scores aggregate with equal weight over instances, then seeds within a dataset--family--budget cell, then cells within a dataset, then datasets. The headline quantity is $\\text{score}=1-\\text{MSE}$ of the diagnostic. A \\emph{skill} is the MSE of a comparator minus the MSE of the diagnostic. The evaluator reports one-sided 2.5th-percentile lower bounds from 200 hierarchical bootstrap replicates that resample cells, seeds and instances within each of the three fixed datasets. These datasets were disjoint, by normalized record hash, from the two search-plane datasets (a hash-based split of German Credit and Pima Diabetes) used to choose the configuration (\\texttt{final\\_resources\\_manifest.json}, \\texttt{search\\_resources\\_manifest.json}). The evaluator's pre-declared assessment treats the diagnostic as informative only if the three skill lower bounds are positive and its score exceeds the permuted control by a positive lower bound (\\texttt{task\\_contract.json}, \\texttt{protocol.promotion\\_rule}).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18050b94-e857-4c8d-b7a8-a11a5c26f668","uuid":"9eb94958-28d7-402c-b55e-6dd0b3c4d8a5","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:32:00.065Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\section{Introduction}\nShapley-value attributions are a standard way to justify an individual prediction of a tabular model in credit, medical and similar settings \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified}. For models without a tractable exact algorithm, they are usually estimated by a stochastic procedure under a fixed budget of model evaluations. Two runs of the same estimator on the same trained model and the same instance therefore return different attribution vectors, and the features that appear in a short list of reasons can change from run to run. The common deployment practice is to run the explainer once and report what it returns. Whether that practice is adequate, and under which conditions, is rarely stated as a checkable claim.\n\nWe study a deliberately narrow version of this question. The explainer is Kernel SHAP with an interventional value function and a 32-row background, run with a budget of $B=4d$ or $B=16d$ coalitions for a model with $d$ features. The decision-relevant object is the top-3 feature set by absolute attribution. We call a single run \\emph{reproducible} to the degree that independent reruns, which differ only in their coalition sampling, return the same top-3 set. This definition ignores background resampling, model retraining and the choice between explainers; those sources of disagreement are outside our scope and we do not measure them.\n\nThe study has two parts. First, we measure how reproducible single runs are at the two budgets, on three tabular datasets and three model families that were held out from the selection of the diagnostic. Second, we ask whether reproducibility can be \\emph{predicted for a given run from that run alone}. A practitioner holds one run, not eight. A self-diagnostic that reads the run's coalition masks, weights and evaluated coalition values and returns a reproducibility estimate $\\rhat\\in[0,1]$ would let the practitioner flag instances whose top-3 set should not be trusted. We evaluate such a diagnostic against three baselines and against a control that keeps all episode-level information but destroys instance-level pairing.\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Shapley estimation.}\nKernel SHAP casts Shapley estimation as a weighted regression over sampled coalitions \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified}. Later work reduces its variance through paired sampling and regression refinements \\citep{Covert2020Improving}, permutation sampling \\citep{Mitchell2021Sampling}, leverage-score sampling with error guarantees \\citep{Musco2024Provably}, and amortized estimators \\citep{Jethani2021FastSHAP}. \\citet{Gupte2025Understanding} compare Shapley approximations empirically on tabular data. These papers make the estimator more accurate or give accuracy guarantees at a chosen budget. We take the budget as given and ask what a user holding one run can say about the reproducibility of its top-3 set, which is a statement about the run rather than about the estimator's average error.\n\n\\paragraph{What a Shapley value means.}\nThe value function defines what is being attributed: interventional or conditional expectations answer different questions \\citep{Sundararajan2019many,Chen2020True,Frye2020Shapley,Kumar2020Problems,Covert2020Explaining}, and additive attributions can misrepresent models with interactions \\citep{Gosiewska2019Do}. We fix an interventional value function with a 32-row background and treat the resulting attribution, not its causal or data-faithful interpretation, as the object whose run-to-run stability is measured.\n\n\\paragraph{Instability and disagreement of explanations.}\nLocal explanations are known to vary under perturbation and between methods \\citep{Ribeiro2016Why,AlvarezMelis2018Robustness,Krishna2022Disagreement}, and \\citet{Yuan2022empirical} study how background size affects SHAP stability for deep models. Our label isolates a different and narrower source of variation, namely the coalition sampling of a single estimator with everything else fixed. This is analogous in spirit to predictive multiplicity, which asks how much predictions vary across equally good alternatives \\citep{Marx2019Predictive}, except that the alternatives here are reruns of an estimator.\n\n\\paragraph{Stability and similarity measures.}\nFeature-selection stability is commonly measured by average pairwise similarity of selected sets, with corrections for chance agreement \\citep{Nogueira2017Stability}, and rank-based comparisons of incomplete lists use measures such as rank-biased overlap \\citep{Webber2010similarity}. We use the plain Jaccard index on top-3 sets because three reasons is a natural unit for a decision explanation. We do not apply a chance correction, which matters when datasets with $d=10$ and $d=20$ are averaged (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}). Uncertainty quantification for Shapley-based importance has been developed at the population level \\citep{Williamson2020Efficient}; we address the per-instance, per-run level.\n\n\\paragraph{Tabular data and models.}\nOur held-out datasets include an ACS income task from the folktables toolkit \\citep{Ding2021Retiring}. Our model families are logistic regression, random forest and a small MLP implemented in scikit-learn \\citep{Pedregosa2012Scikit}. Deep tabular architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021Revisiting} and the comparison of tree ensembles with neural models \\citep{Grinsztajn2022tree} are not part of this study, and exact tree-specific Shapley algorithms \\citep{Lundberg2018Consistent} are not used. Our framing of reproducibility as a measurable property with explicit scope follows the call for rigorous evaluation of interpretability \\citep{DoshiVelez2017Towards}.\n\nThe label is conditional on the particular assessed run, so it can in principle be predicted from that run's data. It measures coalition-sampling noise only. Because it uses $R=8$ reruns it is itself a noisy estimate of the conditional expectation, and we cannot quantify that noise from the retained evidence.\n\n\\paragraph{Diagnostic task.}\nThe diagnostic receives, for each of up to 20 explained instances of a trained model, the assessed run's masks, weights, evaluated values and anchors, plus $d$, $B$, $k=3$ and the model-family name. It returns $\\rhat\\in[0,1]$ per instance. It never sees labels, reruns, model internals or dataset names.\n\n\\subsection{Design rationale}\nKernel SHAP is a weighted regression, so run-to-run variation of $\\phi$ is sampling variation of regression coefficients. The top-3 set changes between runs when this variation is large relative to the gap between the third and fourth largest $|\\phi_j|$. A single run contains information about its own sampling variation: deleting random subsets of its coalition pairs and re-solving the regression shows how sensitive $\\phi$ is to the particular coalitions drawn. Propagating that sensitivity to the top-3 set by simulation gives an estimate of the expected overlap with a rerun, which is what the label measures. Two weaknesses motivate the remaining components. The jackknife is computed from only $B/2$ coalition pairs and is itself noisy, particularly at $4d$; and its spread need not match the rerun spread. We therefore shrink the simulated value toward a budget-specific prior. We hypothesized that this would reduce error most where the label is high and nearly constant (large budget) and that instance-specific information would matter most near ties between the third and fourth feature. We did not run component ablations, so these are design motivations and not tested mechanisms.\n\n\\subsection{Compared predictors}\n\\paragraph{Self-diagnostic (frozen selected configuration).}\nFor each instance, with $F=8$ folds and $P=4$ independent random partitions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Assign complement pairs of coalitions at random to folds, drop one fold at a time, and re-solve the regression to obtain leave-fold-out solutions $\\phi^{(-f)}$. Folds that leave no more than $d$ rows are skipped. Define centered, scaled deviations $\\Delta_f=\\sqrt{(F'-1)/F'}\\,(\\phi^{(-f)}-\\bar\\phi^{(-\\cdot)})$ over the $F'$ valid folds, so that $\\sum_f\\Delta_f\\Delta_f^{\\top}$ is the delete-one-group jackknife covariance.\n\\item Simulate reruns with 1000 common-random-number draws (500 antithetic pairs, seeded by the episode seed). The diagonal simulator uses $\\phi+\\mu\\,\\sigma\\odot\\zeta$ with $\\sigma_j=(\\sum_f\\Delta_{fj}^2)^{1/2}$ and $\\zeta\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I_d)$. The full-covariance simulator uses $\\phi+\\sum_f\\zeta_f\\Delta_f$, which preserves the efficiency constraint. Each simulated top-3 set is compared with the observed one by Jaccard index and averaged. This gives $s_{d,1}$ (diagonal, $\\mu=1$), $s_{d,0.7}$ (diagonal, $\\mu=0.7$) and $s_{f,1}$ (full covariance, $\\mu=1$).\n\\item Compute the margin $z_{\\mathrm{m}}$, the gap between the third and fourth largest $|\\phi_j|$ divided by the jackknife standard error of that gap, averaged over partitions along with the three simulated values.\n\\item Combine: $s=0.5\\,s_{d,1}+0.2\\,s_{d,0.7}+0.3\\,s_{f,1}$ and\n\\begin{equation}\n\\rhat=\\mathrm{clip}_{[0.02,1]}\\Big(0.6\\,s+0.4\\,\\pi(B/d)+0.02\\tanh\\big(z_{\\mathrm m}/3\\big)\\Big),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere the prior $\\pi$ is linear in $\\log_2(B/d)$ between $0.84$ at $B=4d$ and $0.96$ at $B=16d$.\n\\end{enumerate}\nOn any numerical failure the output falls back to $\\pi(B/d)$. The source is in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/selected\\_config\\_program.py} (constants at lines 35--46; functions \\texttt{\\_jackknife\\_cov}, \\texttt{\\_sim\\_jaccard}, \\texttt{\\_margin\\_z}, \\texttt{\\_empirical\\_bayes} and \\texttt{\\_diagnose\\_one}). The final record stores only a tree hash of the evaluated sources, so the above describes the source file of the configuration designated as selected in the search record, not a hash-verified copy.\n\nProvenance of the constants matters for interpretation. The weights $0.5/0.2/0.3$, $0.6$, $0.02$, the fold count and the partition count are hand-set and were not tuned on instance labels. The values $0.84$ and $0.96$ are described in the configuration's notes as aggregate label means by budget from earlier search-plane evaluations (\\texttt{selected\\_config\\_NOTES.md}). The shrinkage weight is therefore a fixed constant, not an estimated empirical-Bayes weight; we use the term \\emph{empirical-Bayes-style} only for the structure of shrinking toward a data-derived prior.\n\n\\paragraph{Baselines and control.}\nAll are scored with the same per-instance squared error $(\\rhat_i-y_i)^2$. \\emph{Always reliable} outputs $\\rhat=1$. \\emph{Constant 0.5} outputs $0.5$. The \\emph{split-half gap} heuristic is computed by the evaluator from the assessed run alone: it solves the regression separately on the two halves of the complement-pair blocks, sets $se_j=|\\phi^A_j-\\phi^B_j|/2$, forms $z=(|\\phi|_{(3)}-|\\phi|_{(4)})/\\sqrt{se_{(3)}^2+se_{(4)}^2}$, and outputs $0.5+0.5\\tanh z$. The \\emph{pairing-permuted control} scores the diagnostic's own outputs against labels after permuting them among the 20 instances of an episode with each of 20 fixed derangements and averaging. It therefore keeps everything the diagnostic knows about the episode, such as its budget, model and dataset level, and removes only the instance-level pairing between $\\rhat$ and $y$. The diagnostic exceeds this control only if its instance-specific variation is informative.\n\n\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\nThe evaluation was run once under a sealed protocol, with the selected configuration frozen beforehand. Datasets are the ACS income task (California, 2018, 1-year person records via folktables; $d=10$; 20{,}000 sampled rows), Give Me Some Credit (OpenML 46929; $d=10$; 20{,}000 sampled rows) and the second half of a hash-based split of Statlog German Credit ($d=20$; 485 rows). Models are scikit-learn logistic regression, a 100-tree random forest and a one-hidden-layer MLP with 64 units, trained on a 70\\% split with train-only median imputation and standardization. For each dataset, family, budget in $\\{4d,16d\\}$ and seed in $\\{9001,9002\\}$ there is one episode of 20 explained test instances, with nine runs per instance (one assessed, eight reruns). That gives 36 episodes and 720 labeled instances. Scores aggregate with equal weight over instances, then seeds within a dataset--family--budget cell, then cells within a dataset, then datasets. The headline quantity is $\\text{score}=1-\\text{MSE}$ of the diagnostic. A \\emph{skill} is the MSE of a comparator minus the MSE of the diagnostic. The evaluator reports one-sided 2.5th-percentile lower bounds from 200 hierarchical bootstrap replicates that resample cells, seeds and instances within each of the three fixed datasets. These datasets were disjoint, by normalized record hash, from the two search-plane datasets (a hash-based split of German Credit and Pima Diabetes) used to choose the configuration (\\texttt{final\\_resources\\_manifest.json}, \\texttt{search\\_resources\\_manifest.json}). The evaluator's pre-declared assessment treats the diagnostic as informative only if the three skill lower bounds are positive and its score exceeds the permuted control by a positive lower bound (\\texttt{task\\_contract.json}, \\texttt{protocol.promotion\\_rule}).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
