{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgvq8LVbG9oHpQPuG1WaH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TTDB16wsKXHWs5n61Wiavg","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'E'\nt=open('paper.tex').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global t\n    assert t.count(a)==1,a[:50]\n    t=t.replace(a,b)\nrep(r\"Shapley-value attributions are a standard way to justify an individual prediction of a tabular model in credit, medical and similar settings \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified}.\",\n    r\"Shapley-value attributions \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified} are used to explain individual predictions of tabular models in credit, medical and similar settings, and practitioner studies report feature attributions as a widely deployed form of explanation \\citep{Bhatt2020Explainable}.\")\nrep(r\"The common deployment practice is to run the explainer once and report what it returns.\",\n    r\"A common practice, which we take as the case of interest, is to run the explainer once and report what it returns.\")\nrep(r\"which is a statement about the run rather than about the estimator's average error.\",\n    r\"which is a statement about the run rather than about the estimator's average error. The closest work on sampled local explainers asks the per-explanation version of this question for LIME: \\citet{Visani2021Statistical} define stability indices from repeated runs, and \\citet{Slack2020Reliable} model the uncertainty of LIME and Kernel SHAP explanations with Bayesian local regressions. Both ask for more runs or a posterior over them. We instead ask whether the top-3 set returned by one run at a fixed budget will be reproduced by a rerun, using only that run's own coalition data and an evaluator that does reruns to supply the label.\")\nrep(r\"Deep tabular architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021Revisiting} and the comparison of tree ensembles with neural models \\citep{Grinsztajn2022tree} are not part of this study,\",\n    r\"Deep tabular architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021Revisiting} are not part of this study,\")\nrep(r\"so that $\\sum_f\\Delta_f\\Delta_f^{\\top}$ is the delete-one-group jackknife covariance.\",\n    r\"so that $\\sum_f\\Delta_f\\Delta_f^{\\top}$ is the delete-one-group jackknife covariance (the delete-a-group jackknife of survey sampling, \\citealp{Kott2001Using}).\")\nrep(r\"we use the term \\emph{empirical-Bayes-style} only for the structure of shrinking toward a data-derived prior.\",\n    r\"we use the term \\emph{empirical-Bayes-style} only for the structure of shrinking an unreliable per-unit estimate toward a prior derived from other data, as in Stein-type estimators \\citep{Efron1973Stein}.\")\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(t)\nE\ngrep -n 'Grinsztajn' paper.tex claims_grounding.json; sed -n 240,260p claims_grounding.json"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":449,"cache_read_input_tokens":74571,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":449},"output_tokens":31,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18050b94-e857-4c8d-b7a8-a11a5c26f668","uuid":"d53960cb-2549-4ef5-b432-dd9189f73a51","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:32:36.348Z","request_id":"req_011Cgvq8Kji8b5X82c8bzrpo"}
